WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:06.080
This video is part of yet another big history
YouTube collaboration, this time talking about

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.490
various events in French history, but more
on that later.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:13.900
As always before talking about the main point
of the video, the Paris commune, we have to

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.622
understand the events that lead up its creation.

00:00:15.622 --> 00:00:16.830
Btw I am going to mispronounce every single
French word in this video I apologize ahead

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.830
of time.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.630
So during the time leading up to 1871 France
was an empire, the Second French Empire to

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.080
be exact, led by Napoleon the third.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.800
The same guy who installed Maximilian 1st
on the Mexican throne but I digress.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.730
So ever since Napoleon the third uncle's defeat
at Waterloo, France was very politically unstable.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.370
Well it was also politically unstable before
then but we don’t have time to get into

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.370
that.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.820
After Waterloo, the Bourbon dynasty was reestablished
in 1815, and outside of the republican leaning

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.690
Parisians this was initially accepted by the
majority of French people who finally wanted

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:54.040
some stability after all of the bloodshed
that happened.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.862
Plus the new bourbon ruler Louis the 18th,
for obvious reasons, made sure to be a more

00:00:58.862 --> 00:01:03.850
liberal monarch than his brother was but when
he died and his son Charles the 10th came

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.110
into power, a lot of the French people became
again very disgruntled with the monarchy as

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.400
Charles was a much more conservative monarch
then his father was.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.440
This angered many people especially in Paris
whose inhabitants were far more republic leaning

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.360
than the rest of the country.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.700
Therefore in 1830 the July revolution started
in Paris, the Parisians where initially supported

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.001
in this by the rest of the country and within
“the three glorious days” the Bourbon

00:01:30.001 --> 00:01:34.710
monarchy was overthrown, however, this is
where the revolutionary movement divided with

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:39.170
most of the Parisians and some other big French
cities wanting a republic and the rest of

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:44.520
France just wanting a less conservative monarch,
therefore once the dust had settled the bourbon

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.770
monarchy was replaced by a more liberal monarchy
under the new Orleanist dynasty.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.689
This establishment of a new, even though liberal
monarchy, was seen as betrayal by the Parisian

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:57.719
as they wanted a republic not a monarchy.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:03.020
Therefore in 1832 they staged another Parisian
rebellion but this time it was unsuccessful

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.049
and so the monarchy remained.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:10.590
But the status quo wouldn’t hold long as
in February 1848 during the year of revolutions,

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:15.020
Parisians rose up again and successfully overthrew
the Orleanist monarchy, forming the Second

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.020
French Republic.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.550
This republic yet again gained a more conservative
character over time as most of ruler France

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.230
were still monarchists.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.760
Seeing the conservative shift of the republic
Parisians rebelled yet again that same year

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.150
known as the June Days uprising.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.760
This was unsuccessful and the conservative,
at least to the Parisians, Napoleon the third

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.590
was elected as the president of the second
French Republic who then four years later

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.940
proclaimed the establishment of the second
French Empire.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.459
During this entire time Parisians became more
and more disgruntled, as they have rebelled

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:49.660
and rebelled only for their dreams of a republic
to be always crushed by the more conservative

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.330
leaning ruler France that wanted a monarchy.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:57.720
Due to this feeling of suppression, during
the second French empire Paris became a hotbed,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.970
even more than it was before, for leading
leftist ideas.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:07.129
By 1870 Paris was full of Marxists, Jacobins,
Anarchists, but also more milder socialists

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.629
and all kinds of other leftists who had a
hard time agreeing on stuff other than that

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:13.120
the monarchy must go.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.470
This is not to say that there were no monarchy
supporters in Paris or that every person who

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.550
supported one of these movements believed
in all of its ideas but many of the working

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:25.989
class people of Paris whose numbers have been
growing steadily due to industrialization,

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:30.750
felt alienated by the monarchy and wanted
a republic, but seeing the years of failure

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.020
to establish a republic meant that many of
these people ended up being drawn to more

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.320
radical ideas as the only way to achieve this
goal.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.459
This movement of Parisian working class people
towards more radical ideas was further exacerbated

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:49.220
by Napoleon's actions towards Paris, not only
due to fears of yet another Parisian revolution

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.049
was Paris denied a city council, a benefit
that many other cities in France had, but

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:59.959
Napoleon also started extensive Parisian building
projects which created large beautiful boulevards

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:04.209
for the rich while destroying a lot of working
class housing in the process.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:08.599
This meant that Paris was well ready for another
insurrection if given the chance and such

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:12.689
a chance came during the 1870 to 71 Franco
Prussian war.

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:17.609
The mood in Paris at the start of the war
was surprisingly one of ultra patriotism,

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:21.880
because the thing that the Parisians hated
even more than the French monarchy were the

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:22.880
Prussians.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.270
However, France was completely stomped by
the Prussians with Napoleon and his 80k strong

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.180
army being defeated and captured at Sedan.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.620
When the news of Napoleon's capture came to
Paris the city wasted no time and another

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:39.120
Parisian revolution established the French
third republic, although at the moment due

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.250
to being in an active war the new provisional
government was just called the Government

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.620
of National defence.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.020
The Prussians were at first not sure whether
the new Parisian Government of National defence

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.521
was legitimate or whether it would continue
with the war but seeing that the government

00:04:53.521 --> 00:04:57.780
quickly started to mobilize new troops across
France and made it clear to the Prussians

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.970
that it was definitely not surrendering the
war resumed with Prussia quickly advancing

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:04.960
and besieging Paris in September 1870.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.310
The French army stationed in Paris tried breaking
the siege the first few months but was unsuccessful

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.220
suffering high losses, and therefore it didn’t
try such a move again.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.160
During this time the new minister of war Léon
Gambetta flew over the Prussian siege of Paris

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.360
in a balloon so he could help mobilize a new
French army outside of Paris with which he

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.540
would later come and relieve the city.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.880
Meanwhile in Paris the situation started to
slowly deteriorate.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:35.979
Many high class people of the city, most of
whom owned much of the shops and industry

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:39.460
within Paris, left before the Prussians siege
started.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.389
This meant that the middle and lower class
people, many of whom couldn’t even afford

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:48.389
to leave the city, were now stuck in a siege
with no work as much of the industry outside

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:51.289
of military manufacturing had been closed.

00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:56.160
Due to this many of the working class people
started to enlist in mass into the national

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.819
guard since that remained the only reliable
paying job within the city.

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:04.020
The national guard was an amature military
force organized by the Government used to

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.060
support the main military in times of need
like the current siege of Paris.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.470
Due to all these new enlistments the national
guard in Paris swelled up to around 300 to

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:19.610
350 thousand men, this was at least twice
as much as the number of professional French

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.440
soldiers in Paris at the time.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.470
The national guard along with many Parisians
was very fervent despite the armies inability

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:31.410
to break the siege, they thought that with
their numbers and zeal they could easily break

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.980
the siege and push the Prussians out of France.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.949
The army however, knowing better wouldn’t
let them attack and so the siege continued

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:42.750
while the food shortage in the city became
more and more problematic.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:47.550
The food situation in Paris was so diare in
fact that there are reports of Parisians eating

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:53.180
horses or even rats to stay alive and one
Parisian even wrote that a desperate woman

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.139
came up to him offering sexual favors for
nothing more than a piece of bread.

00:06:58.139 --> 00:07:02.670
All this, combined with the Parisian and national
guard vehement belief that they could defeat

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.850
the Prussians if only the army and the government
would let them, resulted in growing resentment

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.870
in Paris against the government.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.979
Not only are they not allowing the national
guard to attack the Prussians, they’re not

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:18.419
attacking them themselves, not to mention
that the new mobilized french army that Léon

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:23.090
Gambetta so famously flew on a balloon to
go organize didn’t come to relieve the city

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.240
as promised.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.530
This was because the Prussians defeated all
the new mobilized armies of the third republic

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.970
but due to blocked communication lines Parisians
didn’t know this at first and once they

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:37.600
did find out they blamed the new government
for the losses anyways, some Parisians going

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.470
as far as to say that the new government wanted
to lose to the Prussians.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:46.191
After many Parisian demonstrations and even
an unsuccessful attempt at a coup, the army

00:07:46.191 --> 00:07:50.560
along with the government finally allowed
the national guard to salvie out in January

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.229
to try to take some key points outside of
the city.

00:07:53.229 --> 00:07:57.889
The National guard in a fervent zeal with
no real plan attacked the Prussian lines head

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:00.520
on only to be utterly defeated.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.650
One of these fervent revolutionaries wrote
“At first the National Guard swept the Prussians

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.360
before them, but the mud defeated the brave
son of the people.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.410
They sank into the wet earth up to their ankles,
and unable to get their artillery up on the

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:17.360
hills, they had to retreat.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:24.110
Hundreds stayed behind, lying quietly in death;
these men of the National Guard, men of the

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:29.910
people, artists, young persons, died with
no regrets for their lost lives.

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:36.010
The earth drank the blood of this first Parisian
carnage; soon it will drink more.”

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:40.200
Despite this unsuccessful break through, the
fact that all the French armies were defeated

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.510
and that most of Eastern France was under
Prussian occupation, the Parisians remained

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:49.640
adamant that they could still win the war,
somehow, therefore when the government signed

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:56.420
an armistice with the Prussians 28th of January,
Parisians felt back stabbed by the new republic,

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:01.480
how could they surrender, how could they negotiate
Peace when the jule of France the capital

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:04.420
city still has not fallen to the enemy.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.670
Despite these Parisian feelings the war was
over and the Government of National defence

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:14.580
held an election across France in February
1871 to replace it; this newly elected council

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.070
was meant to negotiate the peace terms with
Prussia and later determine how France would

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:20.790
continue after the war.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:25.060
The council had 650 seats with Paris having
42 of those.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:30.700
All 42 elected members from Paris were leftists
that supported the continuation of the war,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:36.540
however another 400 seats mostly coming from
rural France were former monarchists wanting

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.430
peace, the council selected a guy named Thiers
as the new head.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:45.730
Parisians viewed the election results in horror,
too many times have they created a republic

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:50.910
only for the rest of rural France to form
yet another monarchy, and it was happening

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:51.910
again.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:55.700
And just as you thought that the Parisians
couldn’t hate the new government more than

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:59.170
they already did a series of events made it
possible.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:03.310
Not only did the new government settle on
a peace deal with Prussia which acknowledged

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:08.680
the French defeat an outcome which as stated
was hated by the Parisians but they also allowed

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.480
the Prussian army to hold a parade in Paris.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.950
A city that as far as the Parisians were concerned
wasn’t conquered by the Prussians and therefore

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.740
they had no right to hold a parade in it.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.279
However, the parade happened anyways and the
Parisians were so horrified by this that there

00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:29.640
are accounts that after the parade ended some
Parisians went outside to wash the very street

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:33.910
the Prussians walked on as to cleanse it from
the German stench.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.280
With that said the Prussians didn’t leave.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:41.070
They just camped outside of Paris and waited
until the war reparations defined in the peace

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.290
deal were paid by the French.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:48.160
So as I talk about the following events keep
in mind the Prussians were just there watching

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.160
it all happen.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:54.010
Anyways since the war was over now Thiers
started to implement some new laws to try

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:58.839
to start up the economy of the capital city,
however, these laws seemed more targeted at

00:10:58.839 --> 00:11:01.980
trying to agitate the Parisians than anything
else.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:07.080
For example Thiers lifted the paus on rent,
significantly lowered the pay of the National

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.839
Guard and lifted the moratorium on debts and
pawn shops.

00:11:10.839 --> 00:11:15.279
This was significant because Parisians went
into debt or pawned a lot of their possessions

00:11:15.279 --> 00:11:20.589
during the siege to try to get money to buy
food, thinking that since the moratorium was

00:11:20.589 --> 00:11:25.010
in place on debts and pawnshops they could
just pay the debts and buy their stuff back

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.050
once work resumed in the city.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:31.800
But work didn’t resume in the city in fact
even more shop owners and businessmen who

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.170
weren’t able to leave the city before the
siege left now since Paris during this entire

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:41.490
time was very unstable with protests against
the new government happening on a daily basis

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:46.030
and unpredictable armed national guards were
roaming the streets.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:50.740
Even military manufacturing, the last working
industry in the city, stopped since the war

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:51.740
was over.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:55.350
All of this meant that Thier’s actions that
were meant to get the money flowing again

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.700
through the economy just angered the Parisians
even more since they had no money and there

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.889
was no work to get more money.

00:12:01.889 --> 00:12:06.670
Therefore by March 150 thousand bankruptcies
were declared in the city.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:11.430
With all this happening the last straw was
when Thiers ordered the army to disarm the

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:16.950
national guard starting with capturing it’s
cannons, and ou boy they couldn’t have chosen

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:18.139
a worse target.

00:12:18.139 --> 00:12:23.510
You see these cannons were very symbolic to
the Parisians, not only where they built in

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:28.790
Paris for the war, but they were paid off
by donations from the Parisians so they could

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:30.800
defeat the Prussian siege.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:36.070
Those cannons symbolized everything the Parisians
had fought for in the past few months and

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.620
the government which they hated was now trying
to take the cannons away from them.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:45.290
Naturally a fight broke out between the two
sides and due to the incompetence of the military

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:50.670
officers in charge, the Parisians managed
to win, capturing two military officers and

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:51.790
executing them.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:56.540
Hearing this news and seeing the insurrection
now happening all across Paris Theirs with

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:02.630
the government decided to relocate to Versailles
on the 18th of March.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:08.769
Paris now being abandoned by it’s only governing
body, since remember Paris had no city council

00:13:08.769 --> 00:13:13.079
it was directly controlled by the main French
government, decided to create and appoint

00:13:13.079 --> 00:13:16.590
their own city council since they needed someone
to govern them.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:21.820
They named this new city council the Paris
Commune, this name contrary to popular belief

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:27.279
has nothing to do with communism, commune
just means city or county council in French,

00:13:27.279 --> 00:13:31.300
in fact there are currently over 36 thousand
communes in France.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:35.649
The makeup of this city council which met
for the first time on the 28th of March was

00:13:35.649 --> 00:13:37.399
interesting to say the least.

00:13:37.399 --> 00:13:42.930
It was a hodgepodge of all kinds of leftist
groups, from simple republicans to marxists,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:45.790
jacobins, anarchists, blanquists, etc.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:50.860
Plus even though the council had 92 members
only 60 of them ever bothered to show up to

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:55.980
the meetings, with the rest either recognizing
the Versaille government or not wanting to

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.760
be elected in the first place.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:01.990
One Parisian summarized the members of the
council by saying “the names of the men

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:06.970
in the new government posted, [are] names
so unknown that it seems like a joke”.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:11.910
This statement was quite apt, since many of
the council members were simple workers and

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:16.480
artisense who before this never held any administrative
position in their life.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:21.970
But despite this many Parisians still felt
quite emboldened by one saying “Paris and

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:27.010
the whole country must know the nature, the
reason the aim of the Revolution which is

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:28.860
being carried out.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:33.680
And the responsibility for the mourning, the
suffering and the tragedies of which we are

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:39.810
victims must fall on those who, after having
betrayed France and sold Paris to the foreigner,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:45.579
are pursuing with blind and cruel obstinancy
the ruin of the capital in order to bury in

00:14:45.579 --> 00:14:50.960
the disaster of the Republic and liberty the
dual witness of their treason and their crime.”

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:56.560
So as Parisians established their own city
council, they never actually declared independence

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:01.959
from France, or some kind of a war on France
or even their own independent governance,

00:15:01.959 --> 00:15:06.779
in reality they just established their city
council which they still saw as part of the

00:15:06.779 --> 00:15:10.950
same greater administration and were trying
to decide what to do next.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:16.250
With, yes, some people like the person I just
quoted proposing a communist revolution or

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:21.110
attack on the government in Versaille but
others, which were the majority, were hoping

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:25.440
to negotiate with the Versaille government
on terms such as Paris getting its own city

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:30.709
council, more rights to the workers, the abolishment
of Thiers’s economic laws, etc.

00:15:30.709 --> 00:15:35.209
But Theirs was having none of it, he declined
to negotiate with the commune and quickly

00:15:35.209 --> 00:15:40.430
labeled the insurgency in Paris as a revolution
that needs to be put down, then he started

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.040
massing troops for an attack on the city.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:48.770
This meant that the Paris commune without
actually declaring independence became independent

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:51.170
for a period of less than three months.

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:55.550
This is when most of the laws cited by people
talking about the commune were passed by the

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:56.550
council.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:16:01.529
A vast majority of these never got implemented
due to a lack of time, funds and sometimes

00:16:01.529 --> 00:16:05.370
just apathetic nature of the populace towards
some of these laws.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:10.160
However some notable laws passed outside of
abolishing all Thiers’s economic laws where,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:14.640
the separation of church and state, abolishment
of the death penalty, the continuation of

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:19.990
production in military factories, the restarting
of production in closed and abandoned factories/workshops,

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.070
abolishement of conscription, etc.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:25.920
All of these points however have their caveats.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:30.560
Yes conscription was abolished but now every
healthy working adult was considered as being

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:35.410
automatically part of the National Guard which
was the main military body of the commune.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:40.079
Yes the commune abolished the deaf penalty
but some radical members of the national guard

00:16:40.079 --> 00:16:45.600
did execute several priests and or people
deemed to be spies of the Vareaile government.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:50.140
Yes the workers were given the right to resume
production in abandoned factories and workshops

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:55.390
basically managing it all themselves but the
commune never nationalized anything they still

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:59.700
recognized all the original owners rights
to the factories and businesses.

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:04.120
The commune even negotiated with the Bank
of France to give them a loan to fund all

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:05.120
of this.

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:09.510
By the way the Bank of France was located
in Paris, pretty defenceless and still full

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:13.030
of gold since the Versaille government didn’t
have time to move it.

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:17.429
But despite this the communards decided it
would be better working with the Bank rather

00:17:17.429 --> 00:17:18.860
than nationalising it.

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:23.600
The reason why all these actions of the commune
weren’t actually so communist was because

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.640
the city council as mentioned before was very
divided, with many people following more milder

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:33.980
leftist ideas than others, therefore often
times compromises had to be made, hence the

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:39.360
laws passed by the commune where leftist but
not very marxist, or anarchist.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:45.890
The commune existed in this weird lymbo state
until sunday 21st of May 1871 when Thiers

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:48.830
finally having a big enough army attacked
Paris.

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:54.130
What followed next was brutal street to street
fighting between the French forces and the

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:59.350
National Guard, buildings were lit on fire,
barricades erected, and hundreds of Communards

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:01.450
killed/executed when captured.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:06.090
There was no winning for the commune and many
of them knew this, surrendering the first

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:11.740
chance they got but many were also very radicalized
at this point, refusing to surrender and fighting

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:13.309
to the last breath.

00:18:13.309 --> 00:18:18.559
It took the french forces an entire week to
finally take control of the entire city, a

00:18:18.559 --> 00:18:21.419
week which is remembered today as the Bloody
Week.

00:18:21.419 --> 00:18:27.710
And so 28th of May the Paris commune ended,
existing for only 71 days.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:32.820
There were around one thousand French army
deaths and depending on who you ask around

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.380
5 to 25 thousand communard deaths.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:40.480
Thiers had no mercy for the captured communards,
with some of them being executed and others

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.900
exiled or deported to the furthest French
colonies.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:48.370
Thiers’s very provocative actions towards
Paris after the Franco-Prussian war has led

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:53.460
some historians to believe he agitated the
population on purpose trying to instigate

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:58.730
a revolution that he could then put down and
that way solidify control, others say he was

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:02.960
just very incompatent, and most likely it
was a mixture of both.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:07.820
But one thing Thiers made sure of by the way
he ended the commune was that he created a

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:10.740
giant symbol for the radical leftist in Europe.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:15.560
The way Thiers branded the commune an enemy
from the get go, the fact that he completely

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:20.570
refused negotiating with them and the way
he ended its existence in a Bloody Week meant

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:25.230
that he created thousands of martyrs for the
European radical leftists.

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:30.260
Many leftist thinkers like Marx quickly jumped
on this opportunity and wrote about the commune

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:35.950
as an idealised communist state that was brutally
destroyed by the upper classes, and if only

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:40.380
it would have done certain things differently
like nationalizing the bank it would have

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:45.740
succeeded in taking down the Versaille government,
but none of this was really true.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:51.230
The Paris commune was leftist yes but it definitely
was not some glorious communist state or an

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:56.030
active revolution of the whole proletariat,
nor outside of a few radicals did it want

00:19:56.030 --> 00:20:00.990
to take down the Versaille Government, it
was far from any idealized communist revolution

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:02.970
as portrayed by Marx.

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:07.470
But this did not matter, many of the leftist
movements of the following decades needed

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:11.020
a symbol and the Paris commune became that
symbol.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:15.850
When the bolsheviks took power in Russia they
saw the commune as their predecessor and started

00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:20.190
buying up every possible commune memorabilia
they could get their hands on.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:25.650
An original Communard red flag is buried with
Lenin to this day and another communard red

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:29.890
banner was sent to space by the USSR in 1964.

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:34.760
Due to all this communist idealisation of
the Paris Commune the commune is today remembered

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:40.070
as one of the first comunist states in history
which isn’t really true, it was a new thing

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:47.090
and was on the left side of the political
spectrum but it definitely was not communist.

